// PlutoVG for sysl — 2D vector graphics, bound to https://github.com/sammycage/plutovg.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because PlutoVG depends on nothing: it is C99 with no external
// libraries, and its source is carried in this package. There is no `brew` on a microcontroller and
// no system PlutoVG on bare metal, so vendoring is forced rather than chosen — and it is what `qoi`,
// `qcbor`, `monocypher` and `termbox2` already do.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`design/packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what
// arrived against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       plutovg { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/plutovg", version = "0.2.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.2.0` on this repository.

package {
  name    = "plutovg"
  version = "0.2.0"
}

// **There is no `shim.c`, and that is the headline fact about this binding.**
//
// Every parameter in PlutoVG's public surface is a `float`, an `int`, a pointer or a `const T*` — a
// colour is `const plutovg_color_t*`, a matrix is `const plutovg_matrix_t*`, a rectangle is written
// through a `plutovg_rect_t*` — so the whole library is reachable by declaring it. That is unusual
// enough to be the reason this library was chosen over its competitors.
//
// It is also `float` rather than `double` throughout, which matters on the target this is ultimately
// for: a Cortex-M33's FPU is single-precision, and a library built on `double` would emulate every
// coordinate in software.
//
// **And there is no `requires { headers }` either**, which is the other half of vendoring. The
// constants this binding uses are read out of `plutovg.h` by the C compiler rather than transcribed,
// and that header is carried here beside the C it belongs to — so unlike `cairo` or the SDL3
// packages, there is no directory for a consumer to be told about and no flag to forget.

requires {
  heap  = true
  posix = true
}

// **`heap` is honest and not yet avoidable.** PlutoVG mallocs its canvases, its path storage and its
// glyph caches, and unlike QOI it has no `PLUTOVG_MALLOC` hook to point somewhere else — the
// allocation calls are direct. `plutovg_surface_create_for_data` does keep the *pixels* out of the
// heap, which is the large one and the reason a band-rendering program on a small machine is
// practical at all, but the canvas itself still allocates.
//
// **`posix` is narrower than it looks: it is `write_png` and `font`, and nothing else.** The whole
// drawing surface of this package touches no file system. A program that renders into a buffer it
// owns, loads its font from bytes linked into the binary with `font_from_data`, and never writes a
// PNG, uses no operating system at all — it is declared here because the two functions that do are
// part of the package, and a capability is a property of the module rather than of the call.
//
// Getting either of these down to nothing is a real piece of work — redirecting the allocator would
// need changes upstream — and is worth a card rather than a note. Neither blocks the desktop use this
// package has today.

// **What is NOT here is a `@link` directive**, and the absence is the point of vendoring: the
// implementation is compiled from the sources carried in this package, so there is no external
// library for the linker to be given (`15 §8`). Compare qcbor, which names `m` because its numeric
// coercions reach `pow`.
